From personal experience, Mr. DuBois is full of caca.
Many special purpose processors are not byte-addressable but long-addressable.
(e.g. TMS320C30, TMS320C40)

This means that each address contains not one byte but four. On these
machines:
  printf("%d %d %d %d %d\n",
    sizeof(char), sizeof(int), sizeof(long), sizeof(float), sizeof(double));
produces:
  1 1 1 1 1

That's right: sizeof(char) == sizeof(double). (or 32 bits).

